00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:20.320
in time for the final episode. In 1959 the&nbsp;
British Medical Journal reported on something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.240
that had never been seen before - several patients&nbsp;
with a mysterious condition, something happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.440
inside their blood that doctors didn't think&nbsp;
was possible. Historically, we've understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.160
our blood types to be a constant immovable&nbsp;
fact of our genetics and of our existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.880
But what these doctors found were several&nbsp;
definitive cases of people with an A blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.400
type showing signs of their blood type changing&nbsp;
suddenly having red blood cells temporarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.080
expressing the B blood type antigen. This&nbsp;
acquired B phenomenon, as it's called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.520
was never seen in healthy people, only ever in&nbsp;
people with cancer in their stomach or intestine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.400
or with some other condition where the integrity&nbsp;
of the gastrointestinal wall was compromised.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.920
Over time their blood type would return to&nbsp;
normal, but scientists were not entirely sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.880
what had caused it. In 1972 detectives found&nbsp;
a dismembered body in the River Thames. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:21.520
the forensic investigation one body part recovered&nbsp;
was shown to have O type blood. Later, however, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.480
different part that had remained in the water for&nbsp;
a longer period was identified as having B-type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.560
blood. The blood groups therefore implied the two&nbsp;
parts were not from the same individual. However&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.760
other evidence determined beyond a shadow of a&nbsp;
doubt that the two parts did belong to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.760
This therefore raised the suspicion that the&nbsp;
second part found had an acquired B-type blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.320
What is the common link in these two strange&nbsp;
situations? It's not a change in genetics that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.360
causing the blood type to change, nor is it an&nbsp;
accidental introduction of a different blood type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.760
from somewhere else. It's the work of bacteria&nbsp;
-specifically a certain type of bacterial enzyme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.840
that cleaves or alters certain blood antigens&nbsp;
turning one blood type into another. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.360
first time scientists realized that our blood&nbsp;
types are not so immovable after all. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.800
acquired B phenomenon can certainly raise problems&nbsp;
with typing blood for medicine or with forensic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.720
science, but it could also hold the answer to&nbsp;
a very big problem. If blood can accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.560
be transformed from one type to another, can&nbsp;
it be done on purpose? Can we use this idea to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:36.080
change blood types in order to create universal&nbsp;
donor blood that is always so desperately needed?

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.160
Every two seconds someone in the U.S. needs blood,&nbsp;
but only around 38 percent of the U.S. population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.760
is eligible to give blood or platelets. And&nbsp;
less than ten percent actually do it. And only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.680
seven percent of the population has the so-called&nbsp;
universal donor blood O negative. The red cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.960
reports that on average it only has about six&nbsp;
units of type O blood available for every one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.160
hundred thousand people. But more than twice&nbsp;
that is needed every day. You can start to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.240
the problem here. There is an ever-present need&nbsp;
for blood, especially universal donor blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.000
and only so much available. In a previous video&nbsp;
I discussed the importance of the different blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.800
groups and the ABO and Rh systems. The ABO system&nbsp;
is the most important and your group is determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.320
by the presence of the antigens A and B which are&nbsp;
carried on the surface of your red blood cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:40.800
You can have type A, B, AB, or O - O meaning you&nbsp;
have neither of the A or B antigens. The ABO group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.120
antigens are sugars that work to recognize foreign&nbsp;
cells in the bloodstream. The O blood type lacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:53.680
the A or B antigen and only has what is known as&nbsp;
the H antigen. It's sort of like a blank or base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:00.960
antigen. the A and B antigens are then attached to&nbsp;
this H antigen in the A and B blood types, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.400
only difference between the A and B blood antigens&nbsp;
is a single sugar at the end of each structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.680
People with the A antigen on the surface of&nbsp;
their red blood cells will have an antibody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:18.080
against the B antigen found on the B type blood&nbsp;
cells. And conversely people with the B antigen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.640
will have antibodies against the a antigens found&nbsp;
on A type blood. During transfusion if the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.760
type of blood is injected the red blood cells&nbsp;
in the injected blood will be attacked by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.680
antibodies and the recipient's blood causing them&nbsp;
to clump together or agglutinate this will result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:42.240
in hemolysis or destruction of the red blood cells&nbsp;
which causes serious illness and can be fatal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:49.040
the recipient the rh or rhesus system works in a&nbsp;
similar way if you're missing the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.280
rh antigens you're considered to have a negative&nbsp;
blood type rh negative recipients can only receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.600
rh negative blood but rh positive recipients&nbsp;
can receive either rh positive or negative blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.760
and this is how the eight most common blood types&nbsp;
are formed and because the o negative blood type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.960
does not produce an incompatibility reaction&nbsp;
in any of the eight types it's considered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.600
be the universal donor it's extremely valuable in&nbsp;
medical situations because in a sudden accident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.200
there may not be time to assess a patient's blood&nbsp;
for its type as they're bleeding out if in doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:31.280
o negative blood will pretty much always be a safe&nbsp;
bet but o negative blood can be very hard to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.200
by some solutions to the blood shortage aim to&nbsp;
get more people to donate others aim to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.280
the logistics of blood delivery helping to get it&nbsp;
to the people who need it most but even if more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.680
